# No trailing slash, deliberately.
#
# `node_modules/` matches a DIRECTORY called node_modules and nothing else. A
# symlink called node_modules — which is what a linked local dependency is, and
# what several package managers create — is not a directory, so the pattern
# misses it and the link gets committed. That has happened on this codebase once
# already: a symlink pointing at its own parent went into a commit, and every
# checkout afterwards failed with ELOOP.
node_modules

# The build. Made on the machine that runs it (`bun run build`, which a host
# runs when it installs or updates this module), stamped with the commit, and
# served by `serve.ts` only while that stamp is the checkout's HEAD. A
# checked-in copy would be a second answer to "what does this page say", which
# disagrees with the first the moment somebody edits `src/`.
dist

# Written by `build.ts` for the length of a build and taken away after. Left
# behind by a killed build, it would be what Vite answers at `/`, with no
# ticket in it.
/index.html

# This app's own store. It is somebody's decided material — what they hold a
# change to, and what has been ticked against one — and it belongs to the machine
# the app runs on rather than to any repository this directory happens to be
# sitting in today. That is the whole of what "an app" means here: the directory
# can be copied to another machine, run, and be somebody's checklist.
data
